00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:41.560
So if you look across
IDC, Gartner, all

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:44.790
the other analysts, and read
through all the reports,

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:47.350
really you can summarize
it across four variables

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:48.160
that you see here.

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:49.840
Security and
performance are what

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:51.880
gets talked about the most.

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:56.080
But really, simplicity and cost
are key drivers from a business

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:59.860
perspective as you migrate,
wherever you are on your cloud

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:03.100
journey, as you migrate and
pivot, as trends change,

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:04.494
as customer needs change.

00:02:04.494 --> 00:02:06.160
And so I'm going to
highlight those four

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:09.889
areas across the presentation.

00:02:09.889 --> 00:02:13.250
So just want to give quick
background on AT&amp;T's network.

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:16.610
It's the prime value that's
brought to the table here,

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:17.990
obviously a global network.

00:02:17.990 --> 00:02:19.620
We work through a
number of providers.

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:23.120
We have more than 200
petabytes of data streaming

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:27.360
through our network each day,
very large capital investor

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:32.140
bringing fiber to businesses
around the country.

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:35.030
So now I want to jump
into your options.

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:36.970
As you look to
connect to the cloud,

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:38.880
you have a variety of options.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:40.170
The internet's there.

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:42.430
It gives you, through
some encryption tunnels,

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:45.220
you can get a level of security.

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:47.770
But you're still susceptible
to some of the security

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:49.900
threats such as DDoS.

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:52.060
Also have a level
of performance,

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:53.980
but not guaranteed.

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:56.470
You're, again, susceptible
to the equipment and best

00:02:56.470 --> 00:02:57.830
effort of the internet.

00:02:57.830 --> 00:03:00.400
And what really chimes in
here as well is, again,

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:03.280
cost and the simplicity.

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:06.520
You can get the setup, but you
may have some fixed commitments

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:08.350
and bandwidth requirements
that you may not

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:10.027
be able to easily change.

00:03:10.027 --> 00:03:12.110
Now taking it a step
further, and if you build out

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:15.080
private connectivity, your
performance and security

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:18.580
is greatly increasing just by
getting off of the internet.

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:22.230
Now again, the key here is
cost efficiency and simplicity.

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:24.000
If you want to build
this out, you're

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.100
going to have to overprovision.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:27.830
You need to plan for growth.

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:29.740
So you don't want to
put in small bandwidth.

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:31.660
You want to be able to
add where you think you

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:33.650
might grow and plan for that.

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:36.500
So you need to overprovision,
you need redundancy.

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:39.550
You need to get all the
resources to configure, manage,

00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:41.740
get equipment set up
in these locations.

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:43.570
And then, of course,
we want to connect

00:03:43.570 --> 00:03:46.139
in regions across the
country and across the globe,

00:03:46.139 --> 00:03:47.680
you now have to kind
of exponentially

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:49.450
build towards that.

00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:52.870
So as AT&amp;T approached
this area, we really

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:56.350
looked across those
four areas and how can

00:03:56.350 --> 00:03:58.330
we meet customer
needs and demands

00:03:58.330 --> 00:04:00.820
based on their business speed.

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:03.520
And so what we built out
is NetBond for Cloud.

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:07.080
We preprovisioned
redundant 10 gig circuits

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:09.190
to numerous cloud
providers around the world.

00:04:09.190 --> 00:04:12.070
With Google Cloud, we're
in San Jose and Chicago

00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:13.270
and in Ashburn.

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:16.660
And really what this
allows the customers to do

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:21.760
is not have to plan so far
ahead and make commitments.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:24.070
To get this connection and
security and performance

00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:27.360
and reliability, you
can work through us.

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:30.910
We've done API integration and
used SDN-enabled architecture

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:32.650
and orchestration
on the back end

00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:35.120
to allow for seamless
provisioning to Google

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:37.586
Cloud literally within minutes.

00:04:37.586 --> 00:04:39.460
So you don't have to
plan this ahead of time.

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:42.130
If you're ready to go,
you log into a portal

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:43.600
and you get started.

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:48.610
And as your growth moves
across, you can easily flex.

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:50.890
You can start at 10
megabits per second

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:53.920
and move all the way up into
multi gig bandwidth needs,

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:55.060
again, within minutes.

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:56.710
You don't have to
wait, put in orders,

00:04:56.710 --> 00:04:58.720
and go through that process.

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:03.340
And really tying into
the global AT&amp;T network,

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:07.240
this really allows any IoT
endpoint, any remote office,

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:12.072
remote worker, regional offices,
call location, data centers--

00:05:12.072 --> 00:05:13.780
everything that connects
into the network

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:18.230
can traverse and connect
to your Cloud seamlessly.

00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:21.020
So this is the area I wanted
to highlight a little bit is

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:24.647
similar to Google's model,
we approached this base

00:05:24.647 --> 00:05:25.730
with the customer in mind.

00:05:25.730 --> 00:05:28.790
And so we really worked
across all the verticals

00:05:28.790 --> 00:05:32.007
and industries and every
size customer and asked,

00:05:32.007 --> 00:05:32.840
what are your needs?

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:33.999
How are you migrating?

00:05:33.999 --> 00:05:34.790
How are you moving?

00:05:34.790 --> 00:05:35.810
How are you growing?

00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:38.150
And of course the needs
vary across the verticals

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:40.670
and across different
sized companies.

00:05:40.670 --> 00:05:43.190
But really, we looked at
innovation and developing

00:05:43.190 --> 00:05:45.590
into the platform
in creating features

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:49.340
to allow for the customer
to be flexible as they grow.

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:52.527
So want to highlight
in management space,

00:05:52.527 --> 00:05:54.235
we have a simple,
intuitive website where

00:05:54.235 --> 00:05:58.160
you can log in and manage and
see reporting, set up alerts,

00:05:58.160 --> 00:05:59.580
all that great stuff.

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:02.240
But a lot of customers are
using a custom platform,

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:04.410
or they manage their
cloud workloads as well as

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:04.924
the network.

00:06:04.924 --> 00:06:06.090
They may have a third party.

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:09.390
And so we API enabled all of
the service and user management.

00:06:09.390 --> 00:06:11.690
So we have tens of APIs that
allow you to do everything

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:13.640
you can within the portal.

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:16.640
From a performance perspective,
yes, we're off internet,

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:19.630
and it's inherent in
the infrastructure.

00:06:19.630 --> 00:06:22.760
But we also have
service level agreements

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:25.670
across four metrics, jitter
and latency, data delivery,

00:06:25.670 --> 00:06:26.909
and service availability.

00:06:26.909 --> 00:06:28.700
So we're not just saying
we're really good.

00:06:28.700 --> 00:06:30.020
We back it up.

00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:33.170
We also allow customers
to prioritize traffic.

00:06:33.170 --> 00:06:35.480
If you're using
maybe connections

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:37.330
to back up data at
certain intervals,

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:41.431
we have video conference calls
going over those same links.

00:06:41.431 --> 00:06:42.805
Customers were
asking us, we want

00:06:42.805 --> 00:06:44.388
to be able to
prioritize this traffic,

00:06:44.388 --> 00:06:47.030
and we allow you to
tag those packets.

00:06:47.030 --> 00:06:50.280
We also went in, and
in our architecture,

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:53.720
we had, from certain
verticals such as health care,

00:06:53.720 --> 00:06:57.240
requests for we still
need better performance.

00:06:57.240 --> 00:06:59.000
And so we looked at
it, and again, you

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:01.520
know we've heard a
lot about multicloud

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:03.260
over the last couple of days.

00:07:03.260 --> 00:07:05.220
And really, customers,
are asking I'm moving,

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:07.511
I have data stored here, I
have analytics through here,

00:07:07.511 --> 00:07:10.110
my sales staff's products
are somewhere else.

00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:14.060
So what we did is instead
of routing all that traffic

00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:17.150
from that peering location
back to the customer's network

00:07:17.150 --> 00:07:20.630
or data center and then
back out to the third cloud,

00:07:20.630 --> 00:07:23.460
we inherently route all
traffic to the nearest router.

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:26.870
So if you have some workloads
in Los Angeles and others

00:07:26.870 --> 00:07:29.354
up in the Northwest,
and let's say

00:07:29.354 --> 00:07:30.770
your data center's
in Chicago, you

00:07:30.770 --> 00:07:32.811
don't have to traverse
halfway across the country

00:07:32.811 --> 00:07:33.420
and back over.

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:37.160
It'll just hit the local
router and move on.

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:39.350
From a security
perspective, we also

00:07:39.350 --> 00:07:41.330
got a lot of customer
requests of I

00:07:41.330 --> 00:07:44.030
want to add additional
layers of security.

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:46.950
If there's any malicious
activity in the cloud,

00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:50.460
how do I protect my
network and vice versa?

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:52.850
So we allow for
customers a drop in tens

00:07:52.850 --> 00:07:55.250
of appliances in
between, whether it's

00:07:55.250 --> 00:07:56.540
for performance or security.

00:07:56.540 --> 00:08:00.890
So firewalls, IDS, IPS,
WAN accelerators, anything

00:08:00.890 --> 00:08:04.490
you can really think of, we
allow one-gig, 10-gig ports

00:08:04.490 --> 00:08:06.620
to allow you to bring
in what you need

00:08:06.620 --> 00:08:09.380
and manage all that traffic
between your network

00:08:09.380 --> 00:08:10.700
and the cloud.

00:08:10.700 --> 00:08:13.420
And then again, hitting
on customer experience,

00:08:13.420 --> 00:08:15.290
we really wanted
to make sure it was

00:08:15.290 --> 00:08:18.077
simple and seamless
and working easily

00:08:18.077 --> 00:08:19.160
with the customer's needs.

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:22.250
So we provide a white glove
provisioning treatment.

00:08:22.250 --> 00:08:24.220
Essentially we'll
walk you through it.

00:08:24.220 --> 00:08:26.540
Again, it's a real simple
click-through process.

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:28.587
You can do it by
yourself within minutes,

00:08:28.587 --> 00:08:30.420
but some customers are
still not comfortable

00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:32.152
or they don't have
the expertise.

00:08:32.152 --> 00:08:33.860
We'll jump in and help
you get everything

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:35.030
set up and up and running.

00:08:38.730 --> 00:08:42.390
So another piece a lot
of customers look at

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:44.610
is kind of have a
hybrid cloud use case.

00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:49.200
I have either some systems that
I already have in local data

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:49.706
centers.

00:08:49.706 --> 00:08:51.330
Or maybe I have lots
of workloads there

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:54.060
and I want to migrate some but
I want to leave some there.

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:55.380
So how do we build across that?

00:08:55.380 --> 00:09:00.270
So really leveraging
our colocation partners

00:09:00.270 --> 00:09:05.160
and our own IDCs as well
as our data centers,

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:08.940
and using AT&amp;T MPLS network,
we can bridge that gap

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:11.300
and really provide
from a single provider

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:14.010
your end-to-end connectivity
from a hybrid use

00:09:14.010 --> 00:09:16.900
case or straight cloud use
case, and really, again,

00:09:16.900 --> 00:09:18.960
in a global standpoint.

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:20.737
And so one example
I really wanted

00:09:20.737 --> 00:09:22.320
to highlight-- this
came up recently--

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:24.990
there was a large home
automation company.

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:28.310
They have thousands of end
devices, and they were routing.

00:09:28.310 --> 00:09:30.790
They had their data
aggregation and rules engines

00:09:30.790 --> 00:09:31.680
and two data centers.

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:34.981
One was in San Jose,
one was in Atlanta.

00:09:34.981 --> 00:09:36.480
And they were using
a cloud provider

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:39.670
for storage and analytics
and that type of stuff.

00:09:39.670 --> 00:09:42.060
And so they were not getting
the performance they needed.

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:44.220
They were using a mix
of private connectivity

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:46.950
as well as internet
connectivity.

00:09:46.950 --> 00:09:51.097
And they had some major needs,
a big shift in their strategy,

00:09:51.097 --> 00:09:52.430
and they wanted it to move fast.

00:09:52.430 --> 00:09:54.450
So they came to us,
said can you do this?

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:56.260
We need it in three weeks.

00:09:56.260 --> 00:09:58.680
So with our solution, with
our network, and with NetBond,

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:01.560
we had connectivity
to those IoT solutions

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:02.852
as well as the Cloud providers.

00:10:02.852 --> 00:10:05.101
We went back to them and
said, yeah, we can get you up

00:10:05.101 --> 00:10:06.300
and running today.

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:09.360
So obviously we work at
the speed of your business.

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:11.400
As your needs
change, we can then

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:14.800
flex and pivot accordingly.

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:17.300
So really, to tie
this all together,

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:19.490
going back to the
four components,

00:10:19.490 --> 00:10:21.900
security, being
off the internet,

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:23.880
we have additional
levels of security.

00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:26.180
But we also-- again,
based on your needs--

00:10:26.180 --> 00:10:29.180
we allow to add additional
layers of security.

00:10:29.180 --> 00:10:32.034
And we continually build
within our network as well.

00:10:32.034 --> 00:10:33.950
We have our own kind of
virtualized functions.

00:10:33.950 --> 00:10:36.770
You can add in some
managed firewall services.

00:10:36.770 --> 00:10:41.960
You can add in your own white
box and spin up VNFs as needed.

00:10:41.960 --> 00:10:46.212
From a performance
perspective, we're SLA-backed,

00:10:46.212 --> 00:10:47.420
again, allow you to add more.

00:10:47.420 --> 00:10:49.760
And we continue to look
at our architecture to see

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:51.620
how can we make
those improvements

00:10:51.620 --> 00:10:54.560
and really meet your needs
as your company grows.

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:56.180
But really, the two
things I focus on

00:10:56.180 --> 00:10:59.780
are simplicity and
cost efficiency.

00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:02.060
Wherever you are on
your cloud journey,

00:11:02.060 --> 00:11:05.510
we can help step in and
get you going today.

00:11:05.510 --> 00:11:07.070
You don't need to
make a big plan

00:11:07.070 --> 00:11:09.650
and architect all
these things out.

00:11:09.650 --> 00:11:12.560
Just log in, set it
up, start using it.

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:15.980
You don't need it the
next day, disconnect it.

00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:17.130
There's no commitment.

00:11:17.130 --> 00:11:20.649
There's no long-term
things to tie you down.

00:11:20.649 --> 00:11:22.190
Let's get started,
let's keep moving,

00:11:22.190 --> 00:11:25.250
and let's work at your speed.

00:11:25.250 --> 00:11:26.220
Thank you.

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:29.615
