So we started with what we have.

00:38:55.260 --> 00:38:57.150
Verizon has a very
large private IP

00:38:57.150 --> 00:38:59.640
network that provides the
security that the customer is

00:38:59.640 --> 00:39:02.020
looking for, provides
the performance

00:39:02.020 --> 00:39:04.590
with our SLA and
class of services,

00:39:04.590 --> 00:39:06.930
and provide the manageability.

00:39:06.930 --> 00:39:08.490
What was missing
is the connectivity

00:39:08.490 --> 00:39:09.840
to the cloud operators.

00:39:09.840 --> 00:39:12.090
So in this particular case,
we are showing the example

00:39:12.090 --> 00:39:13.199
with Google.

00:39:13.199 --> 00:39:15.240
So what we did, we went
to the different location

00:39:15.240 --> 00:39:17.980
where Google has deployed
peering location,

00:39:17.980 --> 00:39:19.800
and we deployed our
own physical router.

00:39:19.800 --> 00:39:21.840
So we have two
routers from Verizon.

00:39:21.840 --> 00:39:23.790
There are two
routers from Google.

00:39:23.790 --> 00:39:26.150
We establish a
pair of 10-gig NNI.

00:39:26.150 --> 00:39:28.410
So like this, we are providing
a redundant connection

00:39:28.410 --> 00:39:31.450
between our network
and Google facilities.

00:39:31.450 --> 00:39:34.440
So when a network in hours to
days want to connect to Google,

00:39:34.440 --> 00:39:37.550
we start by creating an
SCI port on our router.

00:39:37.550 --> 00:39:41.040
We connect it to their private
IP VPN network on one side,

00:39:41.040 --> 00:39:43.410
and through an API that
we have with Google,

00:39:43.410 --> 00:39:46.500
we establish connection
toward their Google project.

00:39:46.500 --> 00:39:47.610
Its all software driven.

00:39:47.610 --> 00:39:49.568
It takes a few minutes
for the whole connection

00:39:49.568 --> 00:39:51.820
to be up and running.

00:39:51.820 --> 00:39:54.160
What we did, too, is that we
extended that connectivity

00:39:54.160 --> 00:39:55.780
to our wireless network.

00:39:55.780 --> 00:39:57.280
A large number of
our customers are

00:39:57.280 --> 00:39:59.380
expanding into the
wireless domain

00:39:59.380 --> 00:40:02.220
either because they are
deploying more and more

00:40:02.220 --> 00:40:03.409
IoT-driven application.

00:40:03.409 --> 00:40:05.200
Maybe they are using
their wireless network

00:40:05.200 --> 00:40:07.240
as a backup to the
wired LAN network.

00:40:07.240 --> 00:40:09.730
We have gateways today that
connect the wireless network

00:40:09.730 --> 00:40:11.180
to the wired LAN network.

00:40:11.180 --> 00:40:13.866
So when they have devices
on the wireless network,

00:40:13.866 --> 00:40:15.490
they can now gain
the same connectivity

00:40:15.490 --> 00:40:17.620
and get access to
the same application

00:40:17.620 --> 00:40:22.000
to the same set of data as
their wired LAN counterpart.

00:40:22.000 --> 00:40:25.270
We also moved away from
the legacy-like environment

00:40:25.270 --> 00:40:26.650
or legacy-like
model where do you

00:40:26.650 --> 00:40:30.520
have to order access and port
and pay monthly recurring

00:40:30.520 --> 00:40:31.600
charges.

00:40:31.600 --> 00:40:35.380
With SCI we are adopting
the same model as the cloud

00:40:35.380 --> 00:40:38.410
operators and have you pay
for the resources you use,

00:40:38.410 --> 00:40:41.200
which in this particular case
is the amount of traffic that

00:40:41.200 --> 00:40:42.400
goes through the port.

00:40:42.400 --> 00:40:45.040
So you're not any more
constrained by a port speed.

00:40:45.040 --> 00:40:47.200
You can burst up and
down based on the needs

00:40:47.200 --> 00:40:50.930
of your application.

00:40:50.930 --> 00:40:53.820
Now we took that design
and we replicated it

00:40:53.820 --> 00:40:55.640
into many locations
across the globe.

00:40:55.640 --> 00:40:58.640
This is showing the connection
we have with Google only.

00:40:58.640 --> 00:41:01.840
So for those of you who are
familiar with Google operation,

00:41:01.840 --> 00:41:04.900
you know that Google offer
different modes of peerings.

00:41:04.900 --> 00:41:06.850
Verizon has been a
partner with Google

00:41:06.850 --> 00:41:09.820
on the carrier peering
option for many years now.

00:41:09.820 --> 00:41:12.340
And then as Google has
introduced their Partner

00:41:12.340 --> 00:41:14.930
Interconnect, we are
deploying separate set of NNI

00:41:14.930 --> 00:41:17.660
to support that
peering option as well.

00:41:17.660 --> 00:41:19.840
You can see from the map
here that in every region

00:41:19.840 --> 00:41:22.780
we have two or more peering
location, providing you

00:41:22.780 --> 00:41:25.690
that way with the geographical
diversity to access your Google

00:41:25.690 --> 00:41:27.376
facilities.

00:41:27.376 --> 00:41:28.750
In the previous
slide I mentioned

00:41:28.750 --> 00:41:30.400
that SCI is usage-based.

00:41:30.400 --> 00:41:33.670
So what you can do right now
is go and deploy your SCI port

00:41:33.670 --> 00:41:35.440
at any one of these locations.

00:41:35.440 --> 00:41:37.434
There is no fixed cost
associated with SCI.

00:41:37.434 --> 00:41:39.100
You are paying for
the amount of traffic

00:41:39.100 --> 00:41:40.480
that goes through the port.

00:41:40.480 --> 00:41:43.060
So you have the flexibility
to deploy your SCI connection

00:41:43.060 --> 00:41:46.270
at many locations, gain
advantage of diversity

00:41:46.270 --> 00:41:47.690
and proximity to your user.

00:41:50.420 --> 00:41:54.410
Now SCI, like many of these
services that Verizon support,

00:41:54.410 --> 00:41:56.990
we have a GUI-based portal
for managing and supporting

00:41:56.990 --> 00:41:58.430
the application.

00:41:58.430 --> 00:42:00.524
But we've been talking
about speed, flexibility,

00:42:00.524 --> 00:42:02.190
and software-defined
network, and you've

00:42:02.190 --> 00:42:04.280
seen it from the other
providers as well.

00:42:04.280 --> 00:42:07.130
Verizon is working on APIs
for all the new services,

00:42:07.130 --> 00:42:09.500
and SCI is one of those
services that's benefiting

00:42:09.500 --> 00:42:11.280
from that development.

00:42:11.280 --> 00:42:13.236
So today we have a
group of SCI commands

00:42:13.236 --> 00:42:15.110
that are available for
you, and we're working

00:42:15.110 --> 00:42:18.310
on more portals 2018-19.

00:42:18.310 --> 00:42:20.900
With the SCI, now you
can script the behavior

00:42:20.900 --> 00:42:22.666
that you expect
from the network.

00:42:22.666 --> 00:42:24.290
If you're, for example,
working with us

00:42:24.290 --> 00:42:26.180
on management tool
for your application

00:42:26.180 --> 00:42:28.610
and you determine that your
application needs have changed

00:42:28.610 --> 00:42:31.716
or evolved, you can now automate
the change of the network

00:42:31.716 --> 00:42:33.590
to accommodate the
changes that are happening

00:42:33.590 --> 00:42:36.940
at your application layer.

00:42:36.940 --> 00:42:38.532
Now to conclude my
presentation, I'm

00:42:38.532 --> 00:42:40.740
going to go through a quick
example of a customer who

00:42:40.740 --> 00:42:42.270
we're working with today.

00:42:42.270 --> 00:42:44.820
It's a large retail
customer that has thousands

00:42:44.820 --> 00:42:46.650
of stores across the US.

00:42:46.650 --> 00:42:48.840
They want to decommission
that data center and move

00:42:48.840 --> 00:42:50.881
their application to the
cloud, and for that they

00:42:50.881 --> 00:42:54.120
decided to partner with Google
to host their application.

00:42:54.120 --> 00:42:56.280
They reach out to us to
provide the connectivity

00:42:56.280 --> 00:42:58.680
between their Google
facilities and their store.

00:42:58.680 --> 00:43:01.530
And for that we deployed two
SCI ports, one in San Jose

00:43:01.530 --> 00:43:03.457
and one in Chicago,
providing them

00:43:03.457 --> 00:43:05.040
like this with the
diversity that they

00:43:05.040 --> 00:43:07.140
need to connect to Google.

00:43:07.140 --> 00:43:09.660
We extended that connection
as well to their distribution

00:43:09.660 --> 00:43:11.520
fleet for our wireless network.

00:43:11.520 --> 00:43:14.210
One of the applications that
they're trying to start up with

00:43:14.210 --> 00:43:17.414
is to track every
transaction in their stores

00:43:17.414 --> 00:43:19.080
so they can know the
amount of inventory

00:43:19.080 --> 00:43:20.710
in each one of these stores.

00:43:20.710 --> 00:43:24.750
And as a result, they can
direct the distribution fleet

00:43:24.750 --> 00:43:27.180
to the adequate store to ensure
that the stores are always

00:43:27.180 --> 00:43:29.590
adequately stocked.

00:43:29.590 --> 00:43:32.260
I talked about SCI
working in conjunction

00:43:32.260 --> 00:43:33.645
with other services.

00:43:33.645 --> 00:43:35.770
So in this particular case,
we are hosting for them

00:43:35.770 --> 00:43:38.910
two virtual firewall, one
with each of the SCI port,

00:43:38.910 --> 00:43:41.410
which will provide them with
an additional layer of security

00:43:41.410 --> 00:43:43.540
and allow them to control
better the traffic that's

00:43:43.540 --> 00:43:47.336
coming in and out of
their corporate network.

00:43:47.336 --> 00:43:49.835
Finally, we're working with
them on a number of application,

00:43:49.835 --> 00:43:52.700
too, to manage the performance
of that application,

00:43:52.700 --> 00:43:55.100
whether into Google
environment, into our network,

00:43:55.100 --> 00:43:57.850
the wireless network, or their
store so they can quickly

00:43:57.850 --> 00:44:00.880
determine if there are changes
or impact on the performance

00:44:00.880 --> 00:44:04.559
and what need to do to remedy
this and ensure that they

00:44:04.559 --> 00:44:06.100
have the performance
they are looking

00:44:06.100 --> 00:44:08.570
for for their application.

00:44:08.570 --> 00:44:10.690
So it's this bundling
of all these services.

00:44:10.690 --> 00:44:12.970
It's that digital supply
chain that Verizon

00:44:12.970 --> 00:44:15.640
is offering to our
customer, occasionally

00:44:15.640 --> 00:44:19.270
partnership with Google so to
help you with your application

00:44:19.270 --> 00:44:20.170
enablement.

00:44:20.170 --> 00:44:23.520
[MUSIC PLAYING]

